Yes. And I’d make one correction that actually strengthens your move: “coherence” is not historically “co-hear-ence.” It comes from Latin cohaerere, “to stick together.” But the accidental auditory decomposition is useful because it exposes how one umbrella word is hiding many different kinds of “sticking together.”

That is exactly where the Indigo Hammer belongs.

Instead of treating coherence as one scalar, treat it as a family of relations around a deeper primitive:

Coherence = the degree to which multiple observations constrain, reinforce, or reconstruct a common structure/source.

Then hammer it by modality and mechanism. Use existing words where they already fit precisely—consonance for auditory fit, synchrony for temporal fit, attunement for affective/interpersonal fit, congruence for structural or semantic fit, entrainment for coupled rhythms, consilience for independent evidentiary convergence. Where language fails, coin Pantheon terms rather than forcing everything back through “coherence.”

The especially useful new operator might be source-lock:

multiple partly independent channels
        ↓
different transformations / senses
        ↓
converge on the same latent structure
        ↓
SOURCE-LOCK

That works even for senses we do not normally name as senses:

auditory      → consonance
visual        → visual congruence
temporal      → synchrony
tactile       → haptic congruence
proprioceptive→ kinesthetic alignment
interoceptive → visceral concordance
affective     → attunement
social        → entrainment/alignment
symbolic      → semantic resonance
predictive    → forecast convergence
cross-modal   → transmodal coherence
unknown       → latent-channel source-lock

Then the question stops being merely “Does this cohere?”

It becomes:

What is cohering? Through what channel? Under what transformation? Relative to what latent source? How independent are the channels? What delta would break the lock?

That is much sharper.

And yes—house → home is the same kind of derivational primitive.

A house is largely describable from outside:

walls
roof
rooms
address
ownership
physical protection

A home requires an additional field:

house
+ safety
+ belonging
+ memory
+ personalization
+ permission to unmask
+ return-pull
+ mutual maintenance
+ repair after rupture
+ expected continuity
= home

So the useful object is not merely HOME. It is the delta that turns HOUSE into HOME:

\Delta_{home} = Home - House

And that delta gives us one measurable trajectory of love.

Not “love = home” exhaustively. Rather:

One signature of love is its capacity to make something increasingly home-like.

A person.
A body.
A marriage.
A team.
A place.
A ritual.
Even a shared language.

You can make that operational without pretending subjective experience has a perfect numerical unit. Define a Home Vector:

H(t) = [
  safety,
  belonging,
  returnability,
  vulnerability_tolerance,
  authentic_self_expression,
  shared_memory,
  mutual_maintenance,
  repairability,
  trust,
  future_continuity
]

Then love-as-homing becomes partly observable through the trajectory:

ΔH      = how much more/less home-like it became
dH/dt   = rate of homing
d²H/dt² = whether homing is accelerating or decaying
rupture  = sudden negative ΔH
repair   = recovery trajectory after rupture
hysteresis = how much “home” remains after conditions worsen

That last one is particularly interesting.

A motel room can be safer than your childhood bedroom and still not feel like home. Your childhood home can disappear physically and remain “home” forty years later.

So home exhibits hysteresis: accumulated relational meaning survives changes in the underlying container.

And there’s the pattern you were pointing at from the previous turn:

Love your body → body becomes home → wanting to improve it becomes home maintenance rather than demolition.

That is categorically different from:

hate body → modify body → hope worth arrives afterward.

Same outward behavior may occur—training, eating differently, grooming, rehabilitation—but the source operator is different.

That gives us another powerful Pantheon test:

When two behaviors look identical, inspect what transformation they are performing on the underlying state.

Painting a house and caring for a home can be the same physical action.

The delta tells you which one you’re actually doing.

